Stride & Base =C2=B7 Weekly for patient runners

ZONE 2 IS A TOOL, NOT A RELIGION

Easy running has had a great few years. Talk-test pace, nasal breathing, hear=
t-rate caps: the message that most of your miles should be easy has finally s=
tuck. Good. Now for the caveats.

1. Zone 2 is a range, not a number. Lab tests put the first lactate threshold=
 anywhere from 65 to 80 percent of max HR depending on the runner. A 150 bpm =
cap that works for one person is junk miles or overreach for another. If you =
can, get tested once; if not, use a 30-minute time trial and the talk test to=
gether.

2. Drift is real. On hot days or late in long runs your heart rate climbs at =
the same pace. Slowing down to hold the cap is fine. Walking every hill to ho=
ld it is usually not; a few minutes above the line won't erase the session.

3. Base building is a phase, not an identity. After 12 to 20 weeks of mostly =
easy volume, most runners benefit more from adding one quality session a week=
 than from adding another hour of easy running.

THIS WEEK'S SESSION
The "base-builder's tempo": 3 x 10 minutes at a comfortably hard effort (you =
can speak in short phrases), 2 minutes easy between. Once a week, for four we=
eks, before any real speed work.

FROM THE MAILBAG
"Does cycling count toward my running base?" Yes, for the aerobic system. No,=
 for the tendons and bones that running loads. Keep at least 70% of your aero=
bic time on your feet if running is the goal.

